          "In Part 4, we solve the multi-tenant admission challenge by deploying Maximum Bipartite Matching via unit flow reductions. Enterprise cloud customers submit diverse bandwidth reservation requests: high-frequency trading firms require ultra-low-latency dedicated fiber channels; streaming media providers require wide-bandwidth content delivery pipelines; and enterprise SaaS platforms require secure cross-region synchronization slots. Because physical fiber spans feature discrete wavelength channels, the broker must allocate discrete, non-fragmented channel slots without overlapping reservations.",
          "The broker models tenant admission as a bipartite graph G = (Tenants union Slots, E), where an edge connects a tenant to a fiber slot if and only if the slot satisfies the tenant's bandwidth magnitude, latency budget, and geographic destination. We reduce this assignment to a maximum flow problem by adding super-source S and super-sink T with unit capacities. By the Integrality Theorem, the resulting maximum flow yields an optimal, conflict-free tenant matching.",
          "Furthermore, our broker leverages K\u00f6nig's Theorem to extract the dual Minimum Vertex Cover and Maximum Independent Set of pending reservation requests. The minimum vertex cover identifies the critical bottleneck tenant-slot pairings causing reservation contention. Concurrently, the maximum independent set reveals the largest possible subset of mutually non-interfering tenant requests that can be scheduled concurrently across secondary fiber links without touching the contested primary spans.",
          "The code below demonstrates our bipartite tenant workload scheduler, proving that Dinic's unit-network acceleration resolves complex multi-tenant cloud reservations in sub-millisecond time with 100% allocation efficiency.",
          "The application of K\u00f6nig's Duality Theorem and Bipartite Matching to multi-tenant cloud workload admission bridges discrete mathematics with enterprise revenue optimization. In modern multi-cloud computing environments, cloud providers must navigate strict Service Level Agreements (SLAs) with severe financial penalty clauses for missed delivery deadlines. When enterprise customers request guaranteed optical channels, treating reservation scheduling as an online greedy allocation invariably strands capacity: small fragmented reservations block large contiguous blocks, leading to high rejection rates for lucrative enterprise workloads. By batching incoming reservation requests into high-frequency scheduling epochs (e.g., every 100 milliseconds) and solving global Maximum Bipartite Matching via unit-network Dinic flows, the EnterpriseBandwidthBroker achieves mathematically maximal resource utilization. Furthermore, the dual Minimum Vertex Cover extracted from the matching reduction provides infrastructure engineering teams with exact hardware demand signals. If the minimum vertex cover heavily concentrates on specific transpacific wavelength channels between Oregon and Tokyo, cloud capacity planning directors know with mathematical certainty that capital expenditure must be allocated to laying additional fiber pairs on that specific route to unlock pent-up enterprise demand.",
          "Moreover, the complementary Maximum Independent Set derived via K\u00f6nig's duality provides a powerful mechanism for non-blocking secondary scheduling. By identifying the largest mutually independent subset of reservation requests that share no common physical resources, the broker can instantly provision these workloads in parallel without risk of contention or optical cross-talk. This mathematical decoupling allows cloud platforms to maximize revenue velocity while strictly honoring multi-tenant performance isolation guarantees across mission-critical enterprise workloads. By grounding tenant admission in rigorous bipartite matching duality, the broker ensures that cloud customers receive deterministic quality-of-service guarantees without fear of noisy-neighbor resource starvation or unpredictable performance degradation during peak hours. Furthermore, enterprise tenants can inspect real-time allocation proofs generated from the matching matrix, verifying that their contracted bandwidth reservations are backed by dedicated optical hardware paths rather than oversubscribed virtual slices. This transparency fosters trust across competitive institutional banking, streaming, and enterprise software ecosystems, ensuring seamless collaboration and optimal operational performance across planetary enterprise applications that drive modern global business and international scientific discoveries across interconnected research computing grids that shape the frontier of human innovation, scientific knowledge, and computational engineering, ensuring that massive AI clusters and high-performance computing facilities operate at peak potential, delivering continuous, uninterrupted computational throughput for global research networks and enterprise technology ecosystems and software platforms."
