          "Remarkably, the Suffix Link Tree of a string S is structurally isomorphic to the Suffix Tree of the reversed string S^R (or the compacted Patricia trie of reversed suffixes). Every state u in the automaton corresponds to an explicit internal node in the reversed suffix tree, and the tree edges represent ancestral suffix inclusions. Suffix tree traversal algorithms can thus be executed directly on the Suffix Automaton without ever materializing Ukkonen's tree.",
          "This duality allows us to compute the exact occurrence count of every substring in linear time. Non-clone states correspond to suffixes of prefixes of S and initially have an occurrence count of 1; clone states have an initial count of 0. By sorting the automaton states in descending order of length (topological sort) and propagating occurrence counts up the suffix link tree: occ[link[u]] += occ[u], every state computes the exact cardinality of its endpos set in O(N) time.",
          "The Suffix Link Tree is the conceptual bridge that unifies Suffix Automata and Suffix Trees. While the forward transitions next[u][c] define a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) whose paths represent substrings, the backward suffix links link[u] define a directed rooted tree whose root is state 0. In this link tree, the parent of state u is the state representing the longest proper suffix of the substrings in u. Moving up the link tree corresponds to stripping leading characters, identical to traversing up suffix links in Ukkonen's Suffix Tree.",
          "The remarkable mathematical duality discovered by Blumer and Crochemore states that the Suffix Link Tree of string S is structurally isomorphic to the Suffix Tree of the reversed string S^R. An edge in the suffix link tree from link[u] to u corresponds to an edge in the reversed suffix tree labeled with the reversed prefix characters. This duality means that any structural query traditionally executed on a Suffix Tree\u2014such as finding the longest common extension or lowest common ancestor\u2014can be answered on the Suffix Automaton without ever allocating memory for a tree node.",
